# i3 config file (v4)
# Please see http://i3wm.org/docs/userguide.html for a complete reference!

# $mod is set by the including file BEFORE `include config.common`:
#   config      (desktop) -> set $mod Mod4  (Super)
#   config-xrdp (RDP)      -> set $mod Mod1  (Alt; native over RDP, survives
#                            xrdp's per-connect keymap reset, no xmodmap needed)

# set default desktop layout (default is tiling)
# workspace_layout tabbed <stacking|tabbed>
workspace_layout tabbed

# Configure border style <normal|1pixel|pixel xx|none|pixel>
default_border normal 4
default_floating_border normal 4

# Hide borders
hide_edge_borders none

# change borders — $mod+u (none) and $mod+y (pixel 4) left i3 with the rest of
# the layout group (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.36); hotkeyd/binds.py owns them.
# notification-history picker (sp019) — quickshell browser over the persistent
# notification store; the user-bus-singleton daemon owns the freedesktop
# Notifications name. (border normal 4 dropped to free $mod+n.)
#
# $mod+n moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with the clipboard picker below — sp020,
# dotfiles-hwds.43. Its i3 spelling lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`,
# which `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` links in when the daemon has to be stopped.

# avoid leaving unfocuse with full screen
popup_during_fullscreen smart

# Font for window titles. Will also be used by the bar unless a different font
# is used in the bar {} block below.
# font xft:URWGothic-Book 11
font xft:Iosevka 11

# Use Mouse+$mod to drag floating windows
floating_modifier $mod

#enable tiling tiling
tiling_drag modifier titlebar

# start a terminal with pre-created tmux session
# terminal binding moved to per-variant config (router pattern):
#   config (desktop) -> wm-launch-terminal st (tmux/project wrapper)
#   config-xrdp (RDP) -> plain st

# kill focused window
bindsym $mod+Shift+q kill

# program launcher moved to per-variant config (router pattern):
#   config (desktop) -> quickshell ; config-xrdp (RDP) -> rofi/dmenu
# (also $mod+p, $mod+Shift+d --release, and the Tab/w switcher grabs below)

# old rofi launcher (replaced by quickshell AppLauncher)
# bindsym $mod+d exec --no-startup-id rofi -show run
# bindsym $mod+d exec --no-startup-id rofi -show run -theme-str 'entry {placeholder:"run";}'


# launch categorized menu

################################################################################################
## sound-section - DO NOT EDIT if you wish to automatically upgrade Alsa -> Pulseaudio later! ##
################################################################################################

# (volumeicon autostart → config.d/native.conf)
# $mod+Ctrl+m (alsamixer) moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with the apps + session
# group — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.46. Its i3 spelling lives in
# `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf` for the panic path.
#exec --no-startup-id pulseaudio
#exec --no-startup-id pa-applet
#bindsym $mod+Ctrl+m exec pavucontrol

################################################################################################

# Screen brightness controls
# bindsym XF86MonBrightnessUp exec "xbacklight -inc 10; notify-send 'brightness upX'"
# bindsym XF86MonBrightnessDown exec "xbacklight -dec 10; notify-send 'brightness downX'"

# Start Applications
# bindsym $mod+Ctrl+b exec terminal -e 'bmenu'
# bindsym $mod+F3 exec ranger
# $mod+F5 (mocp) moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with the apps + session group —
# sp020, dotfiles-hwds.46. It was already a no-op here: mocp is not installed.
# $mod+Ctrl+t (`picom -b`) and its commented `pkill picom` twin were DELETED
# here, not migrated — sp020, dotfiles-c57. This is the shared base, so every
# session loaded it: on WSL and xrdp, which run deliberately WITHOUT a
# compositor (config.d/wsl.conf, config.d/native.conf), the chord offered to
# start one, and doing so is actively harmful there — it was picom on xrdp that
# the compositor-free focus border replaced. The native session still runs
# picom, but from its own autostart (config.d/native.conf, guarded on
# XRDP_SESSION), which is where a per-session compositor belongs.
#
# No fallback entry and no binds.py entry: a deletion is not a cutover. The
# chord is simply gone.
# (router: moved to desktop config) bindsym $mod+Shift+d --release exec ~/.dotfiles/quickshell/qs-start.sh
# clipboard-history picker (sp014) — opens the quickshell picker; choosing a
# row puts that entry on the clipboard. It is a PICKER, not a paste tool:
# nothing is typed or injected into the focused window, you still paste
# yourself. Binding lives in the base config because every platform gets the
# picker (the copyq server and the cross-display feeder that back it are
# platform-specific and live in config.d/); qs-clip.sh derives the target
# session itself from the live quickshell instances, so no DISPLAY is passed
# here — and nothing on this line depends on an overlay var, which would be
# invisible across the include boundary anyway.
# $mod+v moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with $mod+n — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.43. The
# paragraph above still describes what the picker IS and why the binding is
# platform-independent; what changed is only which engine grabs the key. The
# i3 spelling lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf` for the panic path.
# The screenshot group (Print, $mod+Print, $mod+Shift+Print and the region
# capture below) moved to hotkeyd/binds.py — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.39. Its i3
# spelling lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`, which
# `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` links in when the daemon has to be stopped.
# freed for `move left` (vim hjkl navigation)
# bindsym $mod+Shift+h exec xdg-open /usr/share/doc/manjaro/i3_help.pdf
# region screenshot — enters a "screenshot" i3 mode (shown in the bar like
# resize) and launches the live selector: a GDK window shaped down to the
# rubber-band outline, so the real desktop shows through and nothing is frozen
# or dimmed. Drag or tap two corners; the crop lands in ~/Pictures/screenshots
# and its PATH goes on the clipboard.
# See quickshell/qs-screenshot.sh + quickshell/qs-region.py.
#
# Base, not the platform overlay: every X11 session gets it (native :0, WSL and
# desktop xrdp :10) — stranding the bind in native.conf left WSL unable to
# reach it at all (dotfiles-13q).
#
# The mode exists ONLY so the bar paints its hint strip (Bar.qml modeHints).
# qs-screenshot.sh enters it and resets it on every exit path, so the bind does
# NOT set the mode itself — a bind-set mode would be redundant with the
# launcher's, and the launcher is the one that can guarantee the reset.
#
# The selector holds a pointer+keyboard grab, so it receives the keys directly;
# there are no `w` / drag bindings here. The old ones — and the IPC shim script
# they called — existed only because the retired quickshell dock layer could not
# receive arbitrary keys. Both are gone.
#
# Escape stays as a DEAD-OVERLAY FALLBACK: while the selector lives its grab
# swallows Esc, so this never fires. If the selector dies or never starts, the
# grab is gone, Esc reaches i3, and the mode unsticks instead of trapping the
# session (the dotfiles-ux1 failure class).
mode "screenshot" {
    bindsym Escape mode default
    bindsym q mode default
}
# "screenshot-drag" — a SECOND, separately-declared mode the selector itself
# switches into (an `i3-msg mode screenshot-drag` from qs-region.py's own
# `_press`, not a keybind) the instant the user starts drawing a region.
# Unlike nav-move/nav-resize (dotfiles-5u6m), which are PURELY a Bar.qml-
# local string swap driven by held-modifier state and never touch i3's real
# mode at all, this one has to be a REAL i3 mode: qs-focus-border.py is a
# SEPARATE process with no access to qs-region.py's in-memory drag state,
# and i3's mode-change IPC event is the only channel already wired to both
# of them. i3 silently ignores `mode <name>` for any name never declared
# with its own `mode "<name>" {}` block — there is no such thing as an
# ad-hoc, undeclared mode — so this block has to exist for the signal to
# actually take effect, even though (like "screenshot" above) the selector's
# grab means no binding here ever fires for real; it exists in case the
# grab is ever lost mid-drag, so Escape/q still un-sticks the session
# instead of trapping it (same dotfiles-ux1 reasoning as "screenshot").
mode "screenshot-drag" {
    bindsym Escape mode default
    bindsym q mode default
}
# $mod+Shift+s (the region capture this mode paints hints for) is hotkeyd's —
# see the screenshot-group note above. The two MODES stay here: they are not
# keybind groups. qs-screenshot.sh / qs-region.py enter them over i3-msg purely
# so Bar.qml paints its hint strip, and the Escape/q inside them are
# dead-overlay fallbacks. Migrating those would mean the daemon holding grabs on
# bare Escape and q for the length of a capture — the dotfiles-ux1 trap.
# $mod+Ctrl+x (xkill) was DELETED, not migrated — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.49.
# xkill grabs the pointer and severs the X connection of whatever you click,
# which killed the CLIENT rather than the process: a well-behaved app exits, a
# detached one lives on with no window. It was the escalation for a window that
# ignores $mod+Shift+q, and `--release` was there because its pointer grab races
# a chord that is still physically down.
#
# Dropped by decision, against the recommendation to keep it as a fifth
# recovery key: `pkill`/`kill -9` from any terminal does the same job with an
# accurate target, and a crosshair that destroys whatever it touches is a poor
# thing to have one modifier away from `$mod+Ctrl+m`.

# focus_follows_mouse no

# THE DIRECTIONAL GROUP LEFT i3 (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.37).
#
# $mod+hjkl and $mod+arrows (focus), plus the $mod+Shift forms of both (move)
# — 16 chords, owned by `hotkeyd/binds.py` now.
#
# THIS IS THE GROUP TASK 6 SHIPPED AND REVERTED (fd9e9f2). It failed because
# the daemon's table hardcoded Mod4: on :10 $mod is Mod1, so the Mod4 forms
# were FREE and the daemon grabbed and served them while i3 went on serving
# the Mod1 forms — a split invisible on :0, where the two agree. The daemon
# resolves $mod per display now, from this file's own `i3wm.mod` X resource,
# so one table means Mod4 here and Mod1 over xrdp by construction.
#
# The $mod+Shift+<letter> half was the other open risk, since xrdp wraps every
# character it sends in a synthetic Shift. Measured on the live :10 session
# with a real RDP client (dotfiles-hwds.35): six presses, six dispatches, no
# double-fire. A passive grab matches (keycode, mask); it is not a
# modifier-state read, which is what the nav layer's Ctrl-not-Shift rule was
# about.
#
# The `nav` layer is unaffected and still worth having: it does these same
# motions with NO modifier held, and carries a resize sublayer that has no
# global spelling.
#
# The i3 spelling of the whole group lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`
# so `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` gives it back when the daemon is dead or wrong.

# workspace back and forth (with/without active container)
workspace_auto_back_and_forth yes
# bindsym $mod+b workspace back_and_forth
# bindsym $mod+Shift+b move container to workspace back_and_forth; workspace back_and_forth

# THE LAYOUT GROUP LEFT i3 (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.36).
#
# Split orientation ($mod+s / $mod+b / $mod+q), fullscreen ($mod+z), layout
# mode ($mod+t / $mod+e), sticky ($mod+Shift+p), focus parent ($mod+a) and
# the scratchpad pair ($mod+minus / $mod+Shift+minus) are owned by
# `hotkeyd/binds.py` now, together with the two border binds further up. A
# chord bound here AND there double-fires with no BadAccess to warn, so the
# block is deleted rather than commented into a state someone can uncomment.
#
# The commented-out `floating toggle` / `focus mode_toggle` lines that used to
# sit in the middle of this block went with it. They had been dead for years
# and neither engine binds those chords; leaving them behind would have
# implied the group was still partly here.
#
# STILL i3's, on purpose: $mod+Shift+q (kill). Same argument as $mod+Shift+c
# (reload) in the entry point — a key whose job is to deal with a window that
# is ALREADY misbehaving belongs in the engine that cannot lose its grabs.
#
# Not in the group at all, despite sharing keysyms: $mod+Shift+s (region
# screenshot, hotkeyd's) and $mod+Shift+e (exit, hotkeyd's since
# dotfiles-hwds.46). A passive grab matches its modifier mask exactly, so those
# are untouched by the daemon grabbing the bare-$mod forms. $mod+Ctrl+t was
# listed here too until it was deleted outright (dotfiles-c57).
#
# The i3 spelling of the whole group lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`
# so `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` gives it back when the daemon is dead or wrong.

# THE WORKSPACE GROUP LEFT i3 (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.34).
#
# `$mod+1..8` (switch), `$mod+Ctrl+1..8` (move container), `$mod+Shift+1..8`
# (move and follow) and the `$mod+Ctrl+Left|Right` next/prev pair are owned by
# `hotkeyd/binds.py` now — 26 chords, plus the `set $ws_switch` that only
# these lines used. A chord bound here AND there double-fires with no
# BadAccess to warn, so this block is deleted rather than commented into a
# state someone can uncomment.
#
# `$mod+0` deliberately stayed: it opens `mode "$mode_system"` further down,
# and migrating it means porting an i3 mode to a daemon layer rather than
# moving a bind.
#
# The i3 spelling of the whole group lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`
# so `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` gives it back when the daemon is dead or wrong.

# Open applications on specific workspaces
# assign [class="Thunderbird"] $ws1
# assign [class="Pale moon"] $ws2
# assign [class="Pcmanfm"] $ws3
# assign [class="Skype"] $ws5

# Open specific applications in floating mode
for_window [all] title_window_icon on
for_window [all] title_window_icon padding 4px

for_window [all] title_format "%title"
# PrusaSlicer: trim version from title ("*file - PrusaSlicer-2.8.1 based on Slic3r" → "*file - PrusaSlicer")
for_window [all] border normal 4

for_window [title="qs-launcher"] floating enable, border none, move position center
for_window [title="qs-switcher"] floating enable, border none, move position center
for_window [title="qs-projects"] floating enable, border none, move position center
for_window [title="qs-clip"] floating enable, border none, move position center
for_window [title="qs-notif"] floating enable, border none, move position center
# preview window (ft005, sp022): the native qml6 preview client tiles/docks
# into the layout by design — no floating rule; it sits beside the editor
# like a split. No for_window criteria either: qml6's WM_CLASS is shared by
# every qml6-hosted app in the repo, so the `preview` wrapper resolves and
# moves its window by pid instead (sp022 Task 6).
# No for_window rule for the region selector: qs-region.py is a GTK POPUP =
# override-redirect, so i3 never manages it and no criteria could ever match.
# (The rule that used to sit here matched title="qs-region" against the OLD
# quickshell dock, which set no title at all — it never matched anything.)
for_window [title="alsamixer"] floating enable border pixel 1
for_window [class="calamares"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="Clipgrab"] floating enable
for_window [title="File Transfer*"] floating enable
for_window [class="fpakman"] floating enable
for_window [class="Galculator"] floating enable border pixel 1
for_window [class="GParted"] floating enable border normal
for_window [title="i3_help"] floating enable sticky enable border normal
for_window [class="Lightdm-settings"] floating enable
for_window [class="Lxappearance"] floating enable sticky enable border normal
for_window [class="Manjaro-hello"] floating enable
for_window [class="Manjaro Settings Manager"] floating enable border normal
for_window [title="MuseScore: Play Panel"] floating enable
for_window [class="Nitrogen"] floating enable sticky enable border normal
for_window [class="Oblogout"] fullscreen enable
for_window [class="octopi"] floating enable
for_window [title="About Pale Moon"] floating enable
for_window [class="Pamac-manager"] floating enable
for_window [class="Pavucontrol"] floating enable
for_window [class="qt5ct"] floating enable sticky enable border normal
for_window [class="Qtconfig-qt4"] floating enable sticky enable border normal
for_window [class="Simple-scan"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="(?i)System-config-printer.py"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="Skype"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="Timeset-gui"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="(?i)virtualbox"] floating enable border normal
for_window [class="Xfburn"] floating enable
for_window [title="quickshell-notifications"] floating enable, border none, sticky enable, move position 400 60

# switch to workspace with urgent window automatically
for_window [urgent=latest] focus

# ($mod+Shift+c — reload — lives in the ENTRY POINTS, not here: it also has to
# restart the bar, and that exec needs each entry point's own quickshell env.
# See the note there for why a bare reload is not enough.)

# restart i3 inplace (preserves your layout/session, can be used to upgrade i3)
# Bound to $mod+Shift+c in i3/config. $mod+Shift+r is the HAMMER and
# $mod+Ctrl+Shift+r is PANIC — see the block further down. Also available as
#   i3-msg restart

# exit i3 (logs you out of your X session)
# $mod+Shift+e (exit i3, behind the nagbar) moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with the
# apps + session group — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.46. NOT a recovery key despite
# ending the session: nothing about it helps when the WM is misbehaving, which
# is the test $mod+Shift+q / $mod+Shift+c / $mod+Shift+r pass and this fails.

# THE SYSTEM MODE LEFT i3 (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.38).
#
# $mod+0 and the whole `mode "$mode_system"` block — lock, logout, switch
# user, suspend, hibernate, reboot, shutdown — are `hotkeyd/binds.py`'s now,
# as a ONE-SHOT layer. i3 expressed one-shot by appending `, mode "default"`
# to every bind in the block; the daemon declares it once on the layer, so it
# cannot be forgotten on the bind where forgetting leaves a bare `r` on
# reboot.
#
# The `set $mode_system ...` line went with it. It was the mode's NAME, which
# is also the string the bar matched on to draw the hint strip — see the
# fallback file, which keeps both together because an i3 variable does not
# cross a file boundary.
#
# The label was already wrong before the move and is preserved wrong on the
# i3 side: it promises `(Shift+s)hutdown`, but no Shift+s bind ever existed
# and `p` is shutdown. Fixing the text is its own change, not a rider on a
# cutover.
#
# The i3 spelling of the whole thing lives in
# `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf` so `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` gives back the
# ability to lock and shut down when the daemon is dead or wrong. That
# fallback matters more here than for any other group.
# Rename workspace ($mod+Shift+o, `i3-input -F 'name workspace to "%s"'`) was
# DELETED, not migrated — sp020, dotfiles-hwds.49. sp019 had relocated it here
# from $mod+Shift+n to free that key for the notification picker; it is now
# gone from both engines.
#
# What replaces it in practice: workspaces in this setup are NAMED by the
# project registry and reached positionally through ws-switch.nu (the $mod+<n>
# row) and the quickshell projects picker ($mod+p), so an ad-hoc rename prompt
# named a workspace that the number row would then address by position anyway.
# `i3-msg 'rename workspace to "x"'` remains for the rare case.

# Project workspace picker (quickshell) moved to desktop config (router pattern)
# bindsym $mod+p exec --no-startup-id ~/.dotfiles/quickshell/qs-overlay.sh projects

# THE RESIZE MODE LEFT i3 (sp020 / dotfiles-hwds.48). `$mod+r` and the whole
# `mode "resize"` block are hotkeyd's LAYERS["resize"] now — the same treatment
# nav (dotfiles-hwds.16) and the system mode (dotfiles-hwds.38) got, and for the
# same reason: a mode is a layer, and the daemon can express one without needing
# i3 to hold bare h/j/k/l grabs.
#
# The 5/10 split survives the move (letters 5px, arrows 10px). The bar keeps
# painting its hint strip unchanged: the layer is NAMED "resize", so
# ModeBarTheme.resolve maps it to the same `hints["resize"]` row the i3 mode
# triggered, now fed from the daemon's state socket instead of an i3 mode event.
#
# Its i3 spelling lives in `config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf`, block and all, so
# `hotkeyd-panic.sh panic` gives resize back when the daemon is dead or wrong.

# Navigation mode — MIGRATED to hotkeyd (sp020, ft011, dotfiles-hwds.16).
#
# $mod+o and the whole `mode "nav"` block (bare hjkl focus, Ctrl+hjkl move,
# Alt+hjkl resize, q/Escape/Return to leave) now live in hotkeyd/binds.py as
# LAYERS["nav"], together with the twelve `nop nav-*` marker binds that used to
# publish held-modifier state to the bar. The daemon reports the modifier
# directly on its state socket, so the markers have nothing left to encode.
#
# Why this cutover is safe when the first attempt was not (reverted fd9e9f2):
# that attempt used core XGrabKey, and on :10 where $mod is Mod1 the nav resize
# chords ARE i3's global $mod+hjkl focus binds — the daemon took BadAccess on
# all eight and Alt+h fell through to i3. The daemon now grabs via XI2
# (XIGrabKeycode), which the X server keeps in a SEPARATE conflict domain from
# core grabs, and the later grabber wins. Layer chords are grabbed only while
# the layer is active, so the daemon grabs at layer ENTRY — after i3 grabbed at
# config load — and wins; on exit it ungrabs and i3's global focus binds work
# again. Measured on a rebuilt :10 geometry: Alt+h inside nav resizes (width
# 512 -> 461) instead of moving focus.
#
# i3 keeps its global $mod+hjkl focus/move binds. They are NOT migrated and do
# not need to be.
# hotkeyd PANIC BIND (sp020 Task 10, ft011) — the one bind that never migrates.
#
# The Python hotkey layer owns its binds via per-key XGrabKey, and X grabs die
# with the process. So if hotkeyd crashes while holding the bind table, every
# migrated key stops working at once and there is no way to bring it back —
# unless one key is still handled by i3 itself. This is that key.
#
# It used to call `hotkeyd.sh restart`, which was the wrong recovery: a restart
# helps when the daemon is DEAD and not at all when it is alive and WRONG —
# misgrabbing, stuck in a layer, or serving a table whose chords collide with
# i3's. It also reinstates the very table that broke the session. So this bind
# now hands the keyboard BACK to i3: stop the daemon everywhere, link
# i3/config.d/zz-fallback-binds.conf, reload. Resume from a terminal with
# `~/.dotfiles/hotkeyd/hotkeyd-panic.sh resume` — once panic has run i3 owns
# the whole keyboard again, so a terminal is reachable and resume needs no
# emergency chord of its own.
#
# hotkeyd/test-launcher.sh asserts this exact string exists here, so the bind
# and the script cannot drift apart and be discovered mid-outage.
#
# binds.py's validator REFUSES any table that binds this chord, under every
# modifier spelling ($mod, Mod4+, Mod1+) — see binds.RESERVED_CHORDS. A
# display-agnostic token check alone would leave it stealable on one display.
#
# CHORD CHOICE: $mod+Ctrl+Shift+r, and reachability beats accident-resistance.
#
# This was $mod+Shift+F12, chosen to be awkward on the theory that a
# comfortable emergency chord gets pressed by accident. That theory assumed a
# full keyboard. The primary :10 client is an Android RDP client with NO F12
# key, so the one control that recovers a broken keyboard was unreachable on
# the device most likely to need it. An emergency control that cannot be
# pressed is not an emergency control.
#
# $mod+Shift+r previously ran i3 `restart`, then panic, and now the hammer
# below. Panic moved ONE MODIFIER OUT rather than away: it is still reachable
# without an F12 row, which was the whole reason it left $mod+Shift+F12.
bindsym $mod+Ctrl+Shift+r exec --no-startup-id ~/.dotfiles/hotkeyd/hotkeyd-panic.sh panic

# THE HAMMER. One chord for "my session is wrong, reset it" — the intent that
# used to be spread across four chords (i3 restart, i3 reload + bar, bar alone,
# daemon restart-by-terminal). Reload first, then this display's daemon, then
# the bar.
#
# NOT the same thing as panic, which is why they are different chords rather
# than one escalating chord: the hammer RESTARTS the daemon, panic STOPS it and
# gives the keyboard back to i3. If the daemon is the problem, restarting it
# reinstates the problem — that is exactly when you want the other chord, and
# needing to press it is not the moment to discover it shares a key.
#
# i3-OWNED, and it must stay that way. A restart-everything bind that the
# daemon owned could not restart a WEDGED daemon, which is most of why you
# would reach for it. binds.py may not bind this chord for the same reason it
# may not bind panic's.
#
# `hotkeyd.sh restart` takes no display: it falls back to $DISPLAY, so each
# session's i3 restarts only its OWN daemon (:0 and :10 are separate daemons
# with separate grabs). The env pair is spelled out because config.common
# cannot see i3/config's $qsbarenv — i3 scopes variables per file.
#
# THE DOUBLE QUOTES AROUND `sh -c '...'` ARE LOad-BEARING. i3's command parser
# treats `,` and `;` as command separators and does NOT recognise SINGLE quotes
# as quoting — only double. Written with single quotes alone, i3 splits this
# line at the `;`, runs the first half, and tries to parse `env QS_BAR_...` as
# an i3 command, which fails at RUNTIME with "Expected one of these tokens".
# MEASURED both ways: the binding fires either way (i3 stores the command
# string verbatim and parses it on execution), so the symptom is a chord that
# looks bound, logs a binding event, and does nothing.
#
# This bites BINDINGS ONLY. A config-level `exec_always` takes the rest of the
# line, which is why config.d/native.conf's `sh -c 'nitrogen --restore; sleep 1;
# ...'` is fine unquoted — do not "fix" that one by copying this.
#
# `;` and not `&&`: the bar restart must happen whether or not the daemon
# restart succeeded. A wedged daemon is one of the reasons to press this, and
# `&&` would skip the half most likely to still work.
#
# $HOME (not ~) inside the quotes: i3 substitutes only names declared with
# `set`, so $HOME reaches sh untouched and sh expands it — verified end to end.
bindsym $mod+Shift+r reload, exec --no-startup-id "sh -c '$HOME/.dotfiles/hotkeyd/hotkeyd.sh restart; env QS_BAR_INSET_BOTTOM=50 QS_BAR_INSET_AUTO=1 $HOME/.dotfiles/quickshell/qs-start.sh'"

# Lock screen
# $mod+9 (blurlock) moved to hotkeyd/binds.py with the apps + session group —
# sp020, dotfiles-hwds.46. The system layer's `l` (i3exit lock) is the other
# way to lock; both are the daemon's now, and the fallback restores this one.

